        'articles' => [
            'kp_index' => "The Kp index is the world's most widely used measure of global geomagnetic activity. It's calculated by averaging disturbance readings from 13 magnetometer stations spread around the world, then converted onto a quasi-logarithmic scale from 0 to 9.\n\nHow to read it:\n0-2: Very quiet, nothing worth noting.\n3: Unsettled, but still normal.\n4: Active - minor fluctuations, occasional high-latitude aurora.\n5 (G1): Minor storm. Aurora visible in far-northern regions.\n6 (G2): Moderate storm. Aurora visible further from the poles, some satellite drag.\n7 (G3): Strong storm. Aurora visible at mid latitudes, possible power grid voltage issues.\n8-9 (G4-G5): Severe to extreme storm. Widespread power grid problems possible, aurora visible near the equator in extreme cases.\n\nNOAA's Space Weather Prediction Center publishes an official Kp value every 3 hours from real-time station data, plus a faster 'estimated Kp' from a subset of stations for near-real-time monitoring - that's the number this bot shows you.\n\nWhy it matters: Kp won't tell you exactly what will happen at your location, but a rising Kp is always worth watching if you care about auroras, HF radio, or GPS accuracy.",
            'solar_wind' => "Solar wind is a continuous outflow of charged particles - mostly protons and electrons - streaming from the Sun's outer atmosphere (the corona) into space, typically at 300-800 km/s, sometimes over 1,000 km/s during major eruptions.\n\nIt isn't uniform: slow solar wind (300-450 km/s) tends to come from streamers near the Sun's equator, while fast solar wind (500-800+ km/s) escapes through coronal holes - regions where the Sun's magnetic field opens up into space.\n\nWhy it matters for Earth: the solar wind constantly presses on our planet's magnetic field (the magnetosphere), compressing it on the day side and stretching it into a long tail on the night side. When the wind speeds up, or its embedded magnetic field points the right way (see IMF Bz), more energy leaks into the magnetosphere - that's what actually drives geomagnetic storms and auroras, not speed alone.\n\nTypical quiet-time values sit around 300-400 km/s. Sustained speeds above 500 km/s are worth watching, and spikes above 700-800 km/s usually accompany a CME arrival or a fast stream from a coronal hole.",
            'imf_bz' => "IMF Bz is the north-south component of the interplanetary magnetic field - the magnetic field carried along by the solar wind, measured in nanotesla (nT).\n\nEarth's own field points north near the equator. When Bz also points north (positive), the two fields reinforce each other and mostly deflect incoming solar wind - not much happens.\n\nWhen Bz turns south (negative), the interplanetary field lines up opposite to Earth's field. The two can then 'reconnect' on the day side of the magnetosphere, opening a direct channel for solar wind energy and particles to pour into near-Earth space. This is the single most important factor in whether a burst of solar wind actually causes a geomagnetic storm.\n\nRough guide:\nBz above 0: quiet, low storm risk regardless of wind speed.\nBz -5 to -10 nT: minor to moderate disturbances possible.\nBz below -10 nT, especially for hours at a time: strong to severe storm conditions, particularly combined with high solar wind speed.\n\nBz can swing rapidly, sometimes flipping sign several times within an hour as a CME passes - which is why forecasters watch it in near-real time rather than relying on daily averages.",
            'cme' => "A Coronal Mass Ejection (CME) is a massive expulsion of plasma and magnetic field from the Sun's corona, often (but not always) linked to a solar flare or an eruptive prominence.\n\nA large CME can hurl a billion tons or more of solar material into space at speeds from a few hundred to over 3,000 km/s. If aimed at Earth, it typically takes 1 to 3 days to arrive, giving forecasters a rough advance-warning window - unlike the light from a flare, which reaches Earth in about 8 minutes.\n\nWhat happens on impact: the leading edge compresses Earth's magnetosphere, often causing a sudden, sharp jump in the ground magnetic field. What happens next depends heavily on the CME's internal magnetic field - if it points south (negative Bz), a strong geomagnetic storm is likely; if it points north, even a fast, dense cloud can pass almost unnoticed.\n\nCMEs are the main driver behind the strongest geomagnetic storms on record, including the 1859 Carrington Event and the 2003 Halloween storms. NOAA tracks CMEs with coronagraphs and heliospheric imagers from the moment they leave the Sun until they sweep past Earth.",
            'solar_flare' => "A solar flare is a sudden, intense burst of electromagnetic radiation from the Sun's surface, released when magnetic energy stored in the corona - usually above sunspot groups called active regions - is suddenly released, often through magnetic reconnection.\n\nFlares are classified by peak X-ray brightness, measured by GOES satellites, on a logarithmic scale:\nA and B class: background level, no noticeable effect.\nC class: minor, little or no effect on Earth.\nM class: moderate, can cause brief radio blackouts at high latitudes.\nX class: major, can cause wide-area radio blackouts and radiation storms.\n\nEach letter step is a tenfold jump in energy, and the number after the letter (like the 2 in M2.5) gives a finer scale from 1 to 9 within that class - except X-class, which has no upper limit (X20+ flares have been recorded).\n\nA flare's light reaches Earth in about 8 minutes, so radio or radiation effects begin almost immediately - much faster than the days it can take an associated CME to arrive. Flares are often (not always) bundled with a CME, a burst of energetic protons, or both, especially at M and X class.",
            'geomagnetic_storm' => "A geomagnetic storm is a temporary, significant disturbance of Earth's magnetosphere, caused when unusually strong or long-lasting solar wind - often from a CME or a fast stream from a coronal hole - transfers energy into near-Earth space.\n\nDuring a storm, electric currents in the upper atmosphere and magnetosphere intensify, the ring current around Earth strengthens, and the aurora oval expands toward the equator, sometimes dramatically. NOAA tracks storm strength on the G-scale from G1 (minor) to G5 (extreme), based on the Kp index.\n\nPractical effects can include:\n- Aurora visible at unusually low latitudes\n- Increased drag on low-Earth-orbit satellites, affecting their orbits\n- GPS and GNSS positioning errors\n- HF radio propagation problems, especially over the poles\n- In severe cases, voltage instability or damage risk on long-distance power lines\n\nStorms typically build over hours, peak, then take one to several days to fully subside. The most extreme storm on record, the 1859 Carrington Event, was strong enough to set telegraph equipment on fire and produce aurora visible near the equator.",
            'g_storms' => "NOAA's G-scale describes the severity of geomagnetic storms for the public and technical users alike, running from G1 (minor) to G5 (extreme), derived directly from the Kp index.\n\nG1 Minor (Kp=5): Weak power grid fluctuations possible, minor satellite impact, aurora visible at high latitudes.\n\nG2 Moderate (Kp=6): High-latitude power systems may see voltage alarms, some satellite orientation issues, aurora visible further from the poles.\n\nG3 Strong (Kp=7): Voltage corrections may be needed in power systems, false alarms on protection devices, surface charging and extra drag on satellites, aurora visible at mid latitudes.\n\nG4 Severe (Kp=8-9-): Widespread voltage control problems possible, some grid systems may see outages, significant satellite tracking and orientation problems, aurora visible well beyond mid latitudes.\n\nG5 Extreme (Kp=9): Power grids may see widespread voltage control problems and some may collapse entirely, transformers may be damaged, aurora visible near subtropical latitudes.\n\nEach level up the scale is a meaningfully larger real-world impact, not just a bigger number - which is why NOAA issues separate public watches and warnings tied to this scale.",
            'm_class_flare' => "M-class flares sit in the middle of the solar flare scale, between the common C-class flares and the rare, powerful X-class flares. On the logarithmic GOES X-ray scale, an M1 flare releases about ten times more energy than a C1 flare, and one-tenth the energy of an X1 flare.\n\nM-class flares are numbered M1 through M9, with M9 just short of X-class strength.\n\nTypical effects:\n- Brief (minutes-long) radio blackouts affecting HF communication on the sunlit side of Earth, most noticeable at high latitudes and polar flight routes\n- Occasional minor radiation storms if the flare comes with a burst of energetic protons\n- Sometimes (not always) linked to a CME, which can trigger a geomagnetic storm one to three days later if Earth-directed\n\nM-class flares are common during active periods of the solar cycle - the Sun can produce several per week near solar maximum - and usually aren't disruptive enough to cause widespread concern on their own, though a well-aimed M-class CME can still trigger a moderate geomagnetic storm.",
            'x_class_flare' => "X-class flares are the most powerful category of solar flare, capable of releasing as much energy as a billion hydrogen bombs within minutes. The scale has no fixed upper limit - after X9, flares simply get the next number up (X10, X15, and so on).\n\nThe largest ever reliably measured was an X28 flare in November 2003, so powerful it temporarily saturated the GOES sensor measuring it - its true strength may have been even higher.\n\nEffects of a large X-class flare can include:\n- Wide-area, sometimes complete, HF radio blackouts on the sunlit side of Earth, lasting up to an hour or more\n- Degraded GPS accuracy across large regions\n- Increased radiation exposure risk for astronauts and high-altitude polar flight crews\n- If paired with a fast, Earth-directed CME, a severe to extreme geomagnetic storm one to three days later\n\nX-class flares are rare - only a handful per 11-year solar cycle reach the largest sizes - and are watched closely the moment they're detected, since their radiation effects on Earth begin within minutes, well before any associated CME arrives.",
